-- allium: 3
-- store-split.allium
--
-- Scope: The TARGET split of responsibility between the dispatch TUI process
--        and the SpacetimeDB module (spacetime/module/), and the order the
--        code is migrated in. It says which side owns which kind of
--        behaviour. Migration steps land one at a time: M2, M3, M4, M5 and M6 (below)
--        have landed; M7, the last step, is landing (task #53260), and
--        completes the migration when it lands. Until a step lands, the rules in
--        the specs listed under Excludes stay authoritative for what the
--        system does today, and nothing here overrides them. Each step runs
--        its own spec -> tests -> code cycle on the spec that owns the
--        behaviour it moves, and only then rewrites that spec's rules.
--
--        M4's entity and rules (DispatchRequest, below) live here rather than
--        in an owning spec, because the module owns the request row outright.
--        They describe dispatch as it is now; dispatch.allium,
--        mcp-task-tools.allium, epics.allium, tasks.allium and sync.allium
--        are rewritten to match and point back here. M5's entity and rules
--        (SessionRequest, below) live here for the same reason;
--        pr-workflow.allium, mcp-task-tools.allium, epics.allium,
--        tasks.allium, sync.allium and spacetime-seed.allium are rewritten to
--        match and point back here. M7's close (RequestCloseTask, below) and
--        the close kind of SessionRequest live here for the same reason;
--        mcp-task-tools.allium, pr-workflow.allium, tasks.allium,
--        epics.allium, core.allium and sync.allium are rewritten to match
--        and point back here.
-- Includes: The split rule, the "host work stays thin" rule, the
--           request-row model for multi-step work, the version gate, the
--           migration order, the dispatch request row and its lifecycle
--           (M4), the session request row (wrap-up and exit-session) and its
--           lifecycle (M5), update_tasks' done-path close and its close row
--           (M7), and the details still undecided.
-- Excludes: What a host does to provision a claimed dispatch request — the
--           worktree, the tmux window, the launch (dispatch.allium:
--           ProvisionDispatch); what a host does to serve a claimed session
--           request — the rebase (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase) and the
--           window teardown (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown); what a
--           session close writes to the task (pr-workflow.allium:
--           ExitSession, which runs in the module inside RequestExitSession's
--           transaction); the rest of wrap-up and PR handling
--           (pr-workflow.allium);
--           feed execution and routing (feeds.allium, feed-scripts.allium);
--           what a host does after a PR merge (pr-workflow.allium: PrMerged);
--           the store's
--           subscription and connection mechanics, including the asks that
--           deliver dispatch and session requests to their host (sync.allium:
--           SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests,
--           SubscribeToThisHostsSessionRequests); startup checks
--           (startup.allium); the MCP tools (mcp-task-tools.allium).
-- Dependencies: sync.allium holds the version gate's rule
--               (AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt) and the rules version
--               itself (config.rules_version). Other entities and rules are
--               added here, or in the owning spec, by the migration step that
--               implements them.
--
-- Decisions taken with the user (task #53254), recorded so they are not
-- re-litigated:
--   - S1, the split rule. A piece of behaviour stays in the TUI process only
--     if it needs the operator's machine: git, tmux, launching claude, gh,
--     local repo files, or the local embedding model. Every rule about the
--     shared data's shape, consistency or state transitions belongs in the
--     SpacetimeDB module's reducers, so every client gets the same rule
--     rather than each client re-implementing it.
--   - S2, host work stays in the TUI process, and stays thin. The MCP and
--     hook HTTP endpoint, feed script execution and PR polling remain in the
--     TUI process. There is no separate daemon: agents still need a board
--     running. Each of these only performs the local call and forwards the
--     raw result through one reducer call. None of them decides a state
--     change; the module does.
--   - S3, the module drives multi-step work. Dispatch, wrap-up and
--     exit-session start as request rows that any client may write. The
--     module sets each state of the request (for example requested ->
--     provisioning on host X -> running | failed). The host that owns the
--     work reacts through its subscription, does the local work, and reports
--     the outcome by reducer call. A scheduled reducer fails work that its
--     host never reports. That replaces today's in-memory TUI dispatch
--     watchdog (DISPATCH_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT; dispatch.allium's
--     DispatchingTimeout), which only the board that started the dispatch
--     can enforce.
--   - Cross-cutting risk: a board on an old build would apply old rules
--     against a newer module, or fail to apply rules the module now expects
--     a host to follow. Every migration step that moves a rule into the
--     module therefore needs a hard startup version gate: a board whose
--     build predates the step refuses to run against the module rather than
--     running with the old rule.
--   - The version gate (decided in task #53255, which landed it). The module
--     publishes a RULES VERSION, one integer in a one-row table of its own
--     (a table, so a module that predates the gate answers "no such table"
--     rather than a row the board cannot decode), stamped by the module from its
--     own constant whenever a client connects. The board compares it
--     for EQUALITY with the version compiled into the board, at every
--     connection attempt, and refuses to run on a difference (sync.allium:
--     AStoreWithOtherRulesFailsTheAttempt). The number is held twice, in the
--     module crate and in the board, with a test that fails when they
--     differ; both are hand-bumped by every migration step that moves a
--     rule, in the commit that moves it, and by nothing else. Why not the other candidates: the wasm hash
--     changes on every rebuild whether or not a rule moved, and a build
--     version needs a stored minimum that someone also has to bump. Limit:
--     a board built before the gate cannot refuse; it still meets the
--     module's own checks.
--   - Migration order, one follow-up task per step:
--       M2  Pure data checks move into reducers: sub-status legality,
--           parameter checks, the reparent cycle check, repo grouping.
--           LANDED (task #53255): patch_task refuses an illegal sub_status;
--           patch_epic refuses a reparent cycle, a repo-group reparent and
--           feed_append_only with grouping or a feed role; create_task,
--           respawn_phoenix_successor and set_task_epic route into
--           repo-group sub-epics, and patch_task re-routes on a repo_path
--           change; regroup_epic and flatten_epic are reducers. The
--           services are pass-through for these. Checks about the request
--           rather than the data (an empty patch, the feed interval floor,
--           the typed-url pair, a named epic's existence) stay at the
--           service and MCP boundary. The version gate landed with it.
--       M3  Feed routing and reconcile move into the module; the TUI's feed
--           runner posts raw items only.
--           LANDED (task #53256): the ingest_feed reducer takes the parsed
--           items, an additive flag and the caller's identity, and does what
--           src/feed/ingest/ did: pick the strategy from the epic's own
--           feed_role and group_by_repo, filter and route by signals, ensure
--           the role and repo-group sub-epics, group, upsert, delete the stale
--           subtree, clear tasks stranded on the reviews parent, drop closed
--           retired items and recalculate. The board runs the script, resolves
--           repo paths from local clones, and reports the removed worktrees
--           and tmux windows by comparing its view before and after. The board
--           no longer calls the per-step reducers it used to orchestrate
--           (upsert_feed_tasks_additive and, allow-phantom-symbol: deleted in M7,
--           delete_stale_subtree_feed_tasks and drop_closed_retired_feed_items);
--           M3 left them as primitives ingest_feed shared code with, and M7
--           deleted them. The rules version is 2.
--           See feeds.allium: IngestFeedEmission.
--       M4  Dispatch becomes request rows plus the scheduled timeout.
--           LANDED (task #53257): every launch path (the TUI keys,
--           retry-fresh, research, quick dispatch, MCP dispatch_task and the
--           epic chain) calls the request_dispatch reducer, which claims the
--           task, writes a dispatch_requests row naming the requesting host
--           and schedules a dispatch_request_timeouts backstop row. The host's
--           board sees the row through its host-scoped subscription, claims
--           it (claim_dispatch_request), provisions under the row's deadline
--           and reports report_dispatch_provisioned or report_dispatch_failed;
--           the module writes the task's worktree, window and host, or fails
--           the row and releases the claim, and rolls up the epic chain
--           either way. A starting board fails its own provisioning rows
--           (fail_interrupted_dispatch_requests) before serving requested
--           ones; every task delete fails the task's open request. The TUI
--           watchdog (DISPATCH_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT), the board's own claim
--           release and its post-provision patch are gone. register_host now
--           enforces core.allium's LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce (D11). The
--           rules version is 3. Decided with the user:
--           - D1, the row. A NEW table, dispatch_request, one row per request
--             (DispatchRequest below), not columns on the task. It carries a
--             kind column, "dispatch" for now, so M5 adds wrap_up and
--             exit_session requests without a column change. (M5 departed
--             from this by decision and gave its requests their own table:
--             see W1 under M5. The kind column stays, with its one value.)
--             A task holds at
--             most one OPEN request (requested or provisioning) at a time;
--             finished rows (dispatched or failed) stay as history. Columns:
--             id, task_id, kind, host, mode, origin, state, requested_at, claimed_at,
--             finished_at, reason, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree,
--             deadline (D4) and requested_by (D11). reused_worktree is the
--             host's report of whether provisioning reused an existing
--             worktree rather than creating one, false until dispatched
--             (mcp-task-tools.allium: DispatchTaskViaMcp names it). An optional column
--             is stored as "" for absent, not as an Option, and every
--             timestamp comes from the module clock.
--           - D2, the flow. Any client calls the request reducer. The module
--             claims the task atomically (backlog -> running, as today's
--             claim, so DispatchClaimExclusive still holds: a second request
--             for a claimed task is refused) and writes the row. The row's
--             host is ALWAYS THE REQUESTING HOST, never the task's: you
--             request work on a task, you provision it on your own machine.
--             So the module still refuses a task whose worktree is on another
--             machine, exactly as today's claim does. The host's board sees
--             the row through its subscription, claims it (requested ->
--             provisioning), provisions the worktree, tmux window and agent,
--             and reports either dispatched (with the worktree and window; the
--             module writes the task's worktree, tmux_window and host) or
--             failed (with a reason; the module releases the claim
--             conditionally, as today).
--           - D3, the mode. mode stays a column (dispatch | research |
--             quick). The requester may pass it; when it does not, the module
--             derives it by today's rule (a plan -> dispatch, else the research
--             tag -> research, else dispatch). quick is never derived: only
--             quick dispatch names it, for its own prompt variant. The host
--             reads the mode from the row and never re-derives it. MCP, the
--             epic chain and retry-fresh name no mode (dispatch.allium:
--             RetryFresh), so a retried research task stays research.
--           - D4, the timeout. ONE deadline per request, 60 seconds
--             (config.dispatch_request_timeout) counted from requested_at,
--             whatever state the row is in, and the row exposes it. THE HOST
--             ENFORCES IT: provisioning stops at the deadline — any subprocess
--             still running is killed, what the attempt made is rolled back
--             exactly as for a provisioning failure, and the host reports
--             failed with reason "deadline passed" (dispatch.allium:
--             ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline). The module's timer is only a
--             BACKSTOP for a host that is gone or hung: a SpacetimeDB schedule
--             table, one scheduled row per request, cancelled when the request
--             finishes. It fires 10 seconds after the deadline
--             (config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace), so a live host's stop
--             lands first; when it fires on a row still open the module fails
--             the row with reason "timed out" and releases the claim exactly as
--             a reported failure does.
--             The test that holds the host to this is behavioural: a
--             provisioning whose subprocess stalls fails at the row's
--             deadline. (This replaces an earlier idea of a test that the
--             config value is at least the host's subprocess budget.) M5's
--             requests get their own timeout, decided in M5.
--             A dispatched report arriving after the row has failed is
--             refused, and the host tears down what it made. This replaces
--             the TUI watchdog
--             (DISPATCH_WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT, dispatch.allium's former
--             DispatchingTimeout guarantee) and REVERSES its rule that a
--             timeout does not release the claim: a timed-out request now
--             returns the task to backlog. UnprovisionedIndicator's claim-stamp
--             fallback is re-based on the same config value.
--           - D5, catch-up. A requested row is picked up from its host's
--             initial subscription rows when that host's board starts. A row
--             already provisioning when its host restarts is NOT resumed: the
--             host's startup reports it failed ("host restarted"), which
--             releases the claim. There is one board per host: startup
--             restarts the board inside the single dispatch tmux session
--             (startup.allium), so a starting board's provisioning rows are
--             never a live sibling's.
--           - D6, the callers. EVERY launch path moves onto request rows in
--             M4: the TUI dispatch keys, retry-fresh, research, quick dispatch
--             (tasks.allium: CreateQuickTask creates the task, then requests),
--             MCP dispatch_task and the epic auto-dispatch chain
--             (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask, which advances on a
--             refused request and reports AutoDispatchFailed from the row's
--             outcome, not from a direct return). dispatch_task writes the
--             request and waits up to 30 seconds for dispatched or failed,
--             returning today's result shape (worktree, window) or the failure
--             reason; when the wait ends first it returns the request id and
--             its state, and get_task shows the request state. The TUI's
--             DispatchingFeedback follows the row rather than in-memory state
--             alone.
--           - D7, the gate. This moves rules into the module, so the rules
--             version goes from 2 to 3, hand-bumped in the module crate and in
--             the board in the same commit (sync.allium: config.rules_version).
--           - D8, deletion. Deleting a task with an open request fails that
--             request in the same reducer (reason "task deleted") and cancels
--             its timer. The host sees the row fail and tears down whatever it
--             made.
--             Every module reducer that deletes a task row does this:
--             DeleteTask, BatchDelete and the stale-subtree delete in
--             ingest_feed.
--           - D9, origin. The row records which launch path wrote it
--             (user | mcp | epic_chain | quick; user covers the TUI keys,
--             retry-fresh and research). An epic_chain row that fails raises
--             AutoDispatchFailed from the row itself, on the requesting host's
--             board only (epics.allium: ReportChainedDispatchFailure), so a
--             board restart during the chain's wait no longer loses it.
--           - D10, snapshots. dispatch_request and its timer table are
--             in-flight state and are not in any snapshot
--             (spacetime-seed.allium: SharedTable); a restore loses request
--             history, by decision.
--           - D11, who may act for a host. A request makes its host's board
--             run git, tmux and claude, so a client may name a host — as the
--             requester, the claimer or a reporter — only when that host's
--             registry row exists with an owner and the owner is the
--             caller's own identity (caller(), the store's view of who sent
--             the call). A host with no owner yet is refused. The module
--             records the caller on the row as requested_by, and a host's
--             board serves only rows requested_by its own owner, as a second
--             line behind the module's check (dispatch.allium:
--             ProvisionDispatch). The scheduled backstop is the module's own
--             call and keeps its own check. The owner is a fact these checks
--             can rest on because the host registry's own write enforces
--             core.allium's LocalHostOwnerIsWrittenOnce: a caller may name
--             only itself as a host's owner, and an owned host's owner is
--             never rewritten. A NEW host row must name its owner: host ids
--             are public, so an ownerless row would go to whichever caller
--             registered that id next as its own, and a teammate could lock a
--             new machine out of its own host. A legacy row written ownerless
--             before this was enforced may still be adopted by the first
--             caller naming itself. The board registers only once its
--             identity has settled, and always as that identity
--             (sync.allium: RegisterHostOnConnect). LIMIT, by decision: one person's machines
--             share an owner, so one of their machines can still name
--             another of theirs; a teammate cannot. The older reducers that
--             take a host argument (the backlog claim, poll ownership,
--             settings) do not check it yet; that gap is tracked separately.
--           - D12, failures while no board ran. A request its host's board
--             was not running to see fail — the backstop fired while the
--             board was closed — is already failed in the starting board's
--             baseline, and the starting board does NOT raise it: neither
--             FailedRequestReported nor epics.allium's
--             ReportChainedDispatchFailure fires for it, epic_chain rows
--             included, and a chain stalled that way gets no
--             ChainStallMarked. The failed card shows it: the task is back in
--             backlog and the row records the reason. Decided with the user
--             after M4 landed (raised by weed).
--           - Deferred, out of M4's scope: how long finished rows are kept,
--             and whether a board sees other hosts' open requests.
--       M5  Wrap-up and exit-session become request rows; the module owns
--           the conflict sub_status and the close, the host runs the rebase
--           and the teardown.
--           LANDED (task #53258): the wrap_up MCP tool still runs its host-local checks
--           first, then calls the request_wrap_up reducer, which writes a
--           session_requests row. A rebase row is claimed by its host's board
--           (claim_session_request, which clears a Conflict sub_status),
--           served by running the rebase under the row's deadline, and
--           reported with report_session_request_done or
--           report_session_request_failed (the module sets Conflict on a
--           conflict report). A done or pr row needs no host work and is
--           written already done. A done wrap_up row's id is the exit token.
--           exit_session calls request_exit_session, which checks the token
--           against the rows, applies the close (pr-workflow.allium:
--           ExitSession), rolls up the epic chain, runs the epic
--           auto-dispatch chain and writes an exit_session row naming the
--           window to tear down, all in one transaction; the host's board
--           tears the window down and reports. A scheduled backstop
--           (session_request_timeouts, expire_session_request) fails rows
--           whose host never reports; a starting board fails its own running
--           rows (fail_interrupted_session_requests); every task delete fails
--           the task's open row. The in-memory exit token
--           (McpState.exit_tokens), the board's optimistic conflict clear and
--           conflict write, the board's close_session call from
--           exit_session and the board-side chain (auto_dispatch_next) go.
--           The rules version becomes 4. Decided with the user:
--           - W1, the row. A NEW table, session_request (accessor
--             session_requests), plus a schedule table for its backstop
--             (session_request_timeouts), one row per wrap_up or exit_session
--             request (SessionRequest below). This DELIBERATELY DEPARTS FROM
--             D1, which gave dispatch_request a kind column so that M5 could
--             add kinds without a column change: these requests fit neither
--             its states nor its columns. Their lifecycle is requested ->
--             running -> done | failed, not provisioning -> dispatched; they
--             carry an action, a pr url and a token, not a mode, an origin, a
--             worktree or reused_worktree; and their failure releases no
--             claim. Folding them in would leave half of every row's columns
--             and states meaningless for its kind, and make every dispatch
--             reader filter by kind. Columns: id, task_id, kind (wrap_up |
--             exit_session), action (rebase | done | pr), host, requested_by
--             (D11), state (requested | running | done | failed),
--             requested_at, claimed_at, finished_at, reason, pr_url and
--             deadline; and three the flow needs, added while writing the
--             spec and ACCEPTED by the user: wrap_up_request (on an
--             exit_session row, the wrap_up row whose token it consumed),
--             tmux_window (on an exit_session row, the window to tear down)
--             and chained_task (on an exit_session row, the subtask the epic
--             chain requested, if any, which exit_session's response names).
--             An optional column is stored as "" for absent, as in D1, and
--             every timestamp comes from the module clock. A task holds at
--             most one OPEN session request (requested or running) of either
--             kind; finished rows stay as history.
--           - W2, the wrap_up flow. The MCP handler first runs the
--             host-local checks, BEFORE writing anything: the task is
--             wrappable, its worktree names a branch (rebase only), the
--             worktree is committed and the verify command passes
--             (WrapUpRefusesUnfinishedWork). A refusal there writes no row
--             and issues no token. Then it calls request_wrap_up(task, host,
--             action), host being this board's own. The module checks D11,
--             that the task is running or review with a worktree on that
--             host, that it is not review-tagged (pr_review or dependabot:
--             mcp-task-tools.allium's ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp, decided
--             with the user to be the module's; the tool may keep the same
--             check as an early refusal with the same wording), and that the
--             task has no open session request (RequestWrapUp). For action rebase the row is
--             requested; the owning host claims it (requested -> running),
--             which clears a Conflict sub_status in the same transaction, as
--             the optimistic clear does today (HostClaimsSessionRequest);
--             runs the rebase (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); and reports
--             done, or failed with a reason and whether the failure was a
--             rebase conflict. THE MODULE sets sub_status = Conflict on a
--             conflict report (HostReportsSessionRequestFailed, then
--             pr-workflow.allium: FinishTaskConflict). For done and pr there
--             is no host work: the module writes the row already done, in
--             the same call, and schedules no timer.
--           - W3, the token. A done wrap_up row is the proof that
--             exit_session needs, and replaces the in-memory exit token: the
--             token shown to the agent is that row's id. Only the task's most
--             recent done wrap_up row is valid, as a newer token replaces an
--             older one today, and each is consumed once. Unlike the
--             in-memory token it survives a board restart. A LATER DISPATCH
--             VOIDS IT (decided with the user): a token is valid only if its
--             wrap_up row finished after the finished_at of the task's latest
--             dispatched request, so a token from an earlier session of a
--             re-dispatched task cannot close the new session. A voided token
--             is refused with "invalid exit token".
--           - W4, the exit_session flow. exit_session calls
--             request_exit_session(task, token, action, pr_url). The module
--             requires a done wrap_up row for the task whose id is the token,
--             that is the task's most recent, that finished after the task's
--             latest dispatched request (W3), whose action matches, and that
--             no exit_session row has consumed; pr_url is required for pr
--             (RequestExitSession). In the same call it applies the terminal
--             state (rebase or done -> Done, pr -> Review with the pr url;
--             completed_at from the store clock; pr-workflow.allium:
--             ExitSession), rolls up the epic chain, consumes the token, and
--             writes an exit_session row naming the task's host and its tmux
--             window for the teardown. The close is one transaction, so it
--             either took effect whole or not at all: today's "close did not
--             persist but the token is spent" case is gone, and a refused or
--             failed call leaves the token valid for a retry. exit_session
--             does NOT wait for the teardown (decided with the user): the
--             teardown kills the calling agent's own window, so a reply that
--             waited for it would never be read (mcp-task-tools.allium:
--             ExitSessionViaMcp).
--           - W5, the teardown. The host's board claims the exit_session row,
--             kills the window and reports done, or failed with a reason. A
--             failed teardown does NOT revert the close; it is recorded on
--             the row and logged, as kill_session_window swallowed it before
--             M5 (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown). The same host also
--             runs ClosePrOnDone's gh pr close for a close that took a task
--             with a pr url into Done, as close_session did before M5
--             (confirmed as the owner in M7; see "ClosePrOnDone's owner"
--             under M7).
--           - W6, the epic chain. The module owns the epic chain's next
--             auto-dispatch: AutoDispatchNextSubtask's selection runs in
--             request_exit_session's transaction and writes a dispatch
--             request with origin epic_chain naming the same host (epics.allium:
--             AutoDispatchNextSubtask), only for a close that had a wrap_up
--             behind it, as today. The exit_session row records the chained
--             subtask for the response. What stays on the requesting board is
--             ReportChainedDispatchFailure, unchanged.
--           - W7, the timeouts. As D4: ONE deadline per row from
--             requested_at, exposed on the row; THE HOST ENFORCES IT, and the
--             module's timer is only a backstop. A wrap_up rebase row has 120
--             seconds (config.wrap_up_rebase_timeout), an exit_session
--             teardown 30 seconds (config.exit_session_teardown_timeout), and
--             the backstop fires 10 seconds after the deadline
--             (config.session_request_backstop_grace). A row the backstop
--             finds open fails with "timed out". A timed-out wrap_up row
--             leaves the task as it was: the Conflict flag is cleared only by
--             the claim, as today, and not restored. Short budgets on
--             purpose: the wrap_up tool waits on the row, and an agent stuck
--             in a call is worse than one told to try again.
--           - W8, catch-up and restart. As D5: a requested row is picked up
--             from its host's initial subscription rows when the board
--             starts, and a row already running when its host restarts is
--             not resumed: the starting board fails it ("host restarted")
--             (HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests). For an
--             exit_session row the starting board then still kills the
--             window the row names, best effort (pr-workflow.allium:
--             ExitSessionTeardown).
--           - W9, deletion. As D8: deleting a task fails its open session
--             request ("task deleted") in the same reducer and cancels its
--             timer, in every module reducer that deletes a task row
--             (DeleteTask, BatchDelete and the stale-subtree delete in
--             ingest_feed).
--           - W10, snapshots. As D10: session_request and its timer table are
--             in-flight state and are not in any snapshot
--             (spacetime-seed.allium: SharedTable). A restore loses every
--             unconsumed exit token with the rows; the agent calls wrap_up
--             again.
--           - W11, who may act for a host. D11 applies to every reducer that
--             names a host: request_wrap_up (its host argument),
--             request_exit_session (the task's host, which the row names),
--             the claim, both reports and the restart sweep. The row records
--             requested_by, and a board serves only rows requested_by its own
--             owner. The scheduled backstop keeps its own check.
--           - W12, the gate. This moves rules into the module, so the rules
--             version goes from 3 to 4, hand-bumped in the module crate and in
--             the board in the same commit as the code (sync.allium:
--             config.rules_version).
--           - W13, what stays on the board. The host-local pre-checks (W2),
--             the rebase and the teardown, BranchRebased (the host's own
--             repo-sync refresh after a rebase it ran), ClosePrOnDone's gh
--             call (W5), the MCP waits and response text. update_tasks'
--             status="done" path (mcp-task-tools.allium: MarkTaskDoneViaMcp)
--             kept its board-side close and teardown UNTIL M7, by decision;
--             M7 moved it into the module (X1).
--           - Deferred, out of M5's scope as out of M4's: how long finished
--             rows are kept.
--       M6  PR state is derived in the module from the raw gh status the
--           host forwards. The watcher half is moot: task watchers were
--           removed (commit e292acb1) and nothing is left to move.
--           Decisions taken with the user (task #53259):
--           - P1, watchers. Dropped. task-watchers.allium, the watcher
--             service and the notice delivery no longer exist; the dead
--             task_watchers table stays in the module only so a publish is
--             not refused.
--           - P2, one reducer. The host runs gh and posts what it read in
--             raw form (report_pr_status: the state and reviewDecision
--             strings as gh printed them) or that it could not read the PR
--             (report_pr_unreadable). The module parses, validates and
--             derives every task write: merged -> done, closed -> pr_closed,
--             open -> the review sub_status, unreadable -> pr_unreachable.
--           - P3, counters stay on the host. The consecutive-failure counters
--             and the backoff are pacing of this machine's gh calls and are
--             session-scoped on purpose (core.allium: PrPollState). No new
--             table. The give-up THRESHOLD stays a host constant; the module
--             decides only what a give-up report does to the task.
--           - P4, effects after a merge. The module sets the task Done
--             (completed_at, epic rollup) and clears its window. The host,
--             which read the window before posting, then kills it, sends the
--             desktop notice and respawns a phoenix task's split pane. No
--             teardown row: the work is best-effort and the module needs no
--             answer.
--           - P5, the gate. The rules version goes from 4 to 5, hand-bumped
--             in the module crate and in the board in the same commit.
--       M7  Leftover service logic is removed; MCP handlers call reducers
--           directly.
--           LANDING (task #53260): update_tasks' status="done" path closes in
--           the module, through a new close_task reducer (RequestCloseTask
--           below), and a third session request kind, close, carries the
--           host's teardown. patch_task applies the two transition rules the
--           board's service still added on top of a patch (the stop_pending
--           clear on leaving running, the phoenix respawn on entering Done).
--           The board's own epic recalculations, which repeated the module's
--           rollup, go. Managed-epic provisioning becomes the
--           ensure_managed_epics reducer. The three per-step feed reducers M3
--           left behind (named under M3) are deleted. The board's
--           close_session, its perform_close helper
--           and the close_persisted gate go. The rules version becomes 6.
--           Decided with the user:
--           - X1, the update_tasks done path (mcp-task-tools.allium:
--             MarkTaskDoneViaMcp). The tool keeps its request-shape checks at
--             the MCP boundary (status must be the only field; a dispatched
--             agent may not close its own task) and then calls
--             close_task(task_id, host), host being this board's own
--             (RequestCloseTask). In ONE transaction the module applies the
--             terminal Done write — ExitSession's terminal fields with action
--             done (pr-workflow.allium), except that a done -> done re-close
--             keeps the task's completed_at — clears stop_pending when the
--             task leaves running, clears tmux_window, rolls up the epic chain,
--             and respawns a phoenix task that enters Done (tasks.allium:
--             PhoenixRespawn). It issues, checks and consumes NO exit token and
--             runs NO epic chain. It writes a session request of the new kind
--             close ONLY when there is host work: the task had a live
--             tmux_window or carries a pr-typed url. That row has action done,
--             no wrap_up_request, the window the close cleared (absent when
--             the task had none), and as host the task's host if it has one,
--             else the caller's host argument. The row is written only when
--             that host is owned by the caller (D11); otherwise the task is
--             closed with no teardown row, because a window on a teammate's
--             machine is not ours to kill (as before, where the board's local
--             kill of such a window failed silently). The host serves a close
--             row exactly as an exit_session teardown (pr-workflow.allium:
--             ExitSessionTeardown): it kills the window if there is one, runs
--             gh pr close best-effort for a pr-typed url, and reports done or
--             failed; a failed teardown does not revert the close.
--             KillWindowOfFailedExitSession, the teardown timeout
--             (config.exit_session_teardown_timeout) and its backstop, the
--             restart sweep and the task-deletion failure all apply to close
--             rows as to exit_session rows. An OPEN session request on the
--             task (a wrap_up rebase still running, say) is failed with
--             reason "task closed" in the same transaction rather than
--             refusing the close: a stuck wrap_up must not block closing.
--             W1's one-open-row rule still holds, because the old row is
--             failed before the close row is written. EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp
--             is restated to apply to exit_session rows only. The tool waits
--             for nothing, and its response text is unchanged. The board no
--             longer closes: close_session, perform_close and the
--             close_persisted gate are removed, since the module's write is
--             one transaction that either landed whole or not at all.
--           - X2, patch_task owns the transition rules. When a patch moves a
--             task out of running the module clears stop_pending in the same
--             write (core.allium: PendingStopOnlyWhileRunning) unless the
--             patch names stop_pending itself, in which case the patch's value
--             stands (no board patch sets it true), and when a
--             patch moves a task into Done from another status it runs the
--             phoenix respawn in the same transaction (tasks.allium:
--             PhoenixRespawn; the trigger is unchanged: the phoenix flag, a
--             task no feed owns, entering Done). completed_at on entering
--             Done was already stamped by the module. The board no longer
--             stamps, clears or respawns itself; a board that needs the stamp
--             re-reads the task.
--           - X3, epic rollups. The board-side epic status recalculations that
--             repeated the module's own rollup (after hook events, after
--             patch_epic and create_epic, after a close) are removed. The
--             module is the only place that recalculates (epics.allium:
--             EpicStatusRecalculation).
--           - X4, managed epics. Provisioning (epics.allium:
--             ProvisionManagedEpics) moves into one reducer,
--             ensure_managed_epics(reviews_command, reviews_interval_secs,
--             cve_command, cve_interval_secs), so two boards starting together
--             cannot create duplicates. The board still reads and writes the
--             settings and applies the feed-interval floor, which is request
--             shape, and calls the reducer.
--           - X5, decided: NOT IN M7, deferred to follow-up tasks. The
--             learning create and verdict checks (the summary, the scope
--             shape, the "not retrieved" check); work-package validation and
--             the atomicity of its multi-call update; the atomicity of epic
--             create's two writes; the feed-interval floor and the delete
--             pre-checks in the module; and the plain upsert_feed_tasks
--             reducer, which only test seeding still uses. The feed
--             code-citation lint and the embeddings stay on the board: they
--             need the operator's machine (S1).
--           - X6, the gate. This moves rules into the module, so the rules
--             version goes from 5 to 6, hand-bumped in the module crate and
--             in the board in the same commit as the code (sync.allium:
--             config.rules_version).
--           - ClosePrOnDone's owner, an open question since M5, closed as a
--             decision: the host serving the teardown row (exit_session or
--             close) runs gh pr close (W5), reading the url from the task. A
--             row that fails before its host serves it (the backstop, the
--             restart sweep, the task's deletion, a close that fails an
--             earlier row) leaves the PR open: KillWindowOfFailedExitSession
--             kills the window only. ACCEPTED AS BUILT, FLAGGED FOR USER
--             REVIEW.
--           - Deferred past M7, and so beyond the epic: how long finished
--             request rows are kept, whether a starting board kills the window
--             of a teardown row that failed while no board ran, and whether a
--             board sees other machines' requests (the open questions below).
--
-- Design doc: 2026-10-10-tui-store-split-design.md under the superpowers
-- specs directory (a dated snapshot; this spec is the record).

use "./core.allium" as core

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- What a dispatch request asks for. Only dispatch exists. M4 meant M5 to add
-- wrap_up and exit_session here; M5 gave them their own table instead (W1),
-- with their own kind (SessionRequestKind below).
enum RequestKind { dispatch }

-- What a session request asks for (W1): a wrap-up, the close that follows
-- it, or (M7, X1) the teardown of update_tasks' done-path close, which has no
-- wrap-up behind it.
enum SessionRequestKind { wrap_up | exit_session | close }

enum SessionRequestState { requested | running | done | failed }

-- Which agent a dispatch launches. Fixed on the row when it is written and
-- read from the row by the host, never re-derived there (D3). quick is only
-- ever named by its caller (tasks.allium: CreateQuickTask).
enum DispatchRequestMode { dispatch | research | quick }

enum DispatchRequestState { requested | provisioning | dispatched | failed }

-- Which launch path wrote the request (D9). user is the TUI keys, including
-- retry-fresh and research; mcp is dispatch_task; epic_chain is
-- epics.allium's AutoDispatchNextSubtask; quick is quick dispatch.
enum DispatchRequestOrigin { user | mcp | epic_chain | quick }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One request to dispatch one task, from the moment a client asks until the
-- host reports or the timer fails it, and kept afterwards as history. Stored
-- as the module's dispatch_request table. An absent optional field below is
-- stored as "" rather than as an Option; that is storage, not behaviour.
entity DispatchRequest {
    task: core/Task
    kind: RequestKind
    -- The machine that provisions this request, which is always the machine
    -- that asked for it (D2). Its board's subscription delivers the row
    -- (sync.allium: SubscribeToThisHostsDispatchRequests).
    host: core/Host
    -- The identity of the client whose call wrote the request (D11),
    -- written by the module from the call, never by the client. Always
    -- host.owner when the request was written.
    requested_by: core/UserIdentity
    mode: DispatchRequestMode
    origin: DispatchRequestOrigin
    state: DispatchRequestState
    requested_at: Timestamp
    -- Set by HostClaimsDispatch. Null on a row that failed while still
    -- requested.
    claimed_at: Timestamp?
    finished_at: Timestamp when state = dispatched | failed
    reason: String when state = failed
    worktree: core/Worktree when state = dispatched
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow when state = dispatched
    -- Whether provisioning reused a worktree that already existed rather than
    -- creating it, as the host reported it (HostReportsDispatched). Only
    -- provisioning can tell: it measures the directory before acting.
    -- Stored false until then.
    reused_worktree: Boolean

    transitions state {
        requested -> provisioning
        requested -> failed
        provisioning -> dispatched
        provisioning -> failed
        terminal: dispatched, failed
    }

    is_open: state in {requested, provisioning}

    -- ONE deadline, from requested_at, whatever the state (D4). A row that
    -- sat requested for a while before its host claimed it has that much less
    -- time to provision. Not a second timer from claimed_at. Exposed on the
    -- row because the HOST enforces it (dispatch.allium:
    -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline); the module's own timer
    -- (DispatchRequestTimesOut) is the backstop.
    deadline: requested_at + config.dispatch_request_timeout

    @invariant OnlyTheClaimedRequestProvisions
        -- dispatch.allium's DispatchClaimExclusive, restated for the request
        -- row. A request is written only by the same transaction that claims
        -- its task (RequestDispatch), so a request that is refused wrote
        -- nothing and provisions nothing. Only the board whose claim of the
        -- row was accepted (HostClaimsDispatch) provisions it; a second board
        -- on the same host, or a redelivered row, is refused by the state
        -- check. And a row that has finished cannot be reported on again, so a
        -- worktree provisioned after its request timed out is never recorded
        -- on the task. Not an expression because it constrains the order of
        -- writes, not any single state.
}

-- One wrap_up, exit_session (M5) or close (M7) request for one task, from the
-- moment the module accepts it until it is done or failed, and kept afterwards
-- as history. Stored as the module's session_request table. An absent optional
-- field below is stored as "" rather than as an Option; that is storage, not
-- behaviour.
entity SessionRequest {
    task: core/Task
    kind: SessionRequestKind
    -- The wrap-up action: on a wrap_up row the one asked for, on an
    -- exit_session row the one its token was issued for, on a close row
    -- always done (X1).
    action: core/WrapUpMode
    -- The machine holding the task's worktree and window, whose board does
    -- the host work. Its board's subscription delivers the row (sync.allium:
    -- SubscribeToThisHostsSessionRequests). On a close row: the task's host,
    -- or the closing board's own host when the task has none (X1).
    host: core/Host
    -- The identity of the client whose call wrote the request (W11, D11),
    -- written by the module from the call. Always host.owner when the request
    -- was written.
    requested_by: core/UserIdentity
    state: SessionRequestState
    requested_at: Timestamp
    -- Set by HostClaimsSessionRequest. Null on a row written done (a wrap_up
    -- with action done or pr) and on a row that failed while still requested.
    claimed_at: Timestamp?
    finished_at: Timestamp when state = done | failed
    reason: String when state = failed
    -- On an exit_session row with action pr: the PR url the close recorded on
    -- the task. Null otherwise, a close row included: the host reads a close
    -- row's PR url from the task, as it does for an exit_session row with
    -- action rebase or done (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSessionTeardown).
    pr_url: String?
    -- On an exit_session row: the wrap_up row whose id was the token, which
    -- this row consumed (W3). Null on a wrap_up row and on a close row.
    wrap_up_request: SessionRequest?
    -- On an exit_session or close row: the window the close cleared from the
    -- task, which the host tears down. Null on a wrap_up row, and on a close
    -- row for a task that had no window (written only for its PR url, X1).
    tmux_window: core/TmuxWindow?
    -- On an exit_session row: the subtask the epic chain requested in the
    -- same transaction (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask), or null when
    -- the chain stopped. Null on a wrap_up row and on a close row, which
    -- runs no chain.
    chained_task: core/Task?

    transitions state {
        requested -> running
        requested -> failed
        running -> done
        running -> failed
        terminal: done, failed
    }

    is_open: state in {requested, running}

    -- Whether the row needs its host to do anything. A wrap_up with action
    -- done or pr is written already done and never does (W2). A close row is
    -- written only when there is host work (X1).
    has_host_work: kind = exit_session or kind = close or action = rebase

    -- ONE deadline, from requested_at, whatever the state (W7, as D4).
    -- Exposed on the row because the HOST enforces it (pr-workflow.allium:
    -- WrapUpRebase, ExitSessionTeardown); the module's own timer
    -- (SessionRequestTimesOut) is the backstop. Stored "" on a row with no
    -- host work, which is never open and has no timer. A close row is a
    -- teardown and takes the teardown's budget (X1).
    deadline:
        if kind = wrap_up: requested_at + config.wrap_up_rebase_timeout
        else: requested_at + config.exit_session_teardown_timeout

    -- The exit token this row is, while it is one (W3): a done wrap_up row
    -- for its task with no newer done wrap_up row beside it (newer by
    -- requested_at, ties broken by row id), finished after the task's latest
    -- dispatched request (a later dispatch voids it), and with no
    -- exit_session row that consumed it.
    is_valid_token:
        kind = wrap_up and state = done
        and not DispatchRequests.any(d =>
            d.task = task and d.state = dispatched
            and d.finished_at >= finished_at)
        and not SessionRequests.any(r =>
            r.task = task and r.kind = wrap_up and r.state = done
            and r.requested_at > requested_at)
        and not SessionRequests.any(r =>
            r.kind = exit_session and r.wrap_up_request = this)

    @invariant OnlyTheClaimedSessionRequestIsServed
        -- As OnlyTheClaimedRequestProvisions above. Only the board whose
        -- claim of the row was accepted (HostClaimsSessionRequest) runs the
        -- rebase or the teardown; a second board on the same host, or a
        -- redelivered row, is refused by the state check. A row that has
        -- finished cannot be reported on again, so a rebase that finished
        -- after its row timed out issues no token. Not an expression because
        -- it constrains the order of writes, not any single state.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- How long a dispatch request may stay open, measured from requested_at.
    -- The host stops provisioning at that point and reports failed; the
    -- module fails a row still open then as a backstop
    -- (DispatchRequestTimesOut). A module config value, read by the host
    -- from the row's deadline rather than from a copy of its own. Shorter
    -- than the host's worst-case subprocess budget (several sequential git
    -- calls, each bounded by SUBPROCESS_TIMEOUT) on purpose: the deadline
    -- bounds the dispatch, and a subprocess still running at it is killed.
    -- Dispatch only; M5's requests get their own.
    dispatch_request_timeout: Duration = 60.seconds

    -- How long after the deadline the module's backstop timer waits before
    -- failing a row still open (DispatchRequestTimesOut). Long enough for a
    -- live host's own stop at the deadline, rollback included, to land
    -- first with "deadline passed"; "timed out" is then only ever the reason
    -- for a host that is gone or hung.
    dispatch_request_backstop_grace: Duration = 10.seconds

    -- How long a wrap_up rebase request may stay open, measured from
    -- requested_at (W7). The host stops the rebase at that point and reports
    -- failed (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); the module fails a row still
    -- open then as a backstop (SessionRequestTimesOut). Read by the host from
    -- the row's deadline. A wrap_up with action done or pr has no host work
    -- and no deadline.
    wrap_up_rebase_timeout: Duration = 120.seconds

    -- How long an exit_session request's teardown may stay open, measured
    -- from requested_at (W7). The close itself is already applied by then;
    -- this bounds only the host's window kill (pr-workflow.allium:
    -- ExitSessionTeardown). A close row (M7, X1) is held to the same budget.
    exit_session_teardown_timeout: Duration = 30.seconds

    -- How long after a session request's deadline the module's backstop
    -- timer waits before failing a row still open (SessionRequestTimesOut).
    -- As dispatch_request_backstop_grace: long enough for a live host's own
    -- stop at the deadline to land first.
    session_request_backstop_grace: Duration = 10.seconds
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
------------------------------------------------------------

-- == Dispatch requests (M4) ==

rule RequestDispatch {
    when: DispatchRequested(task, requester, origin, mode?)

    requires: task.status = backlog
    -- Today's claimable_by, unchanged: the request is provisioned on the
    -- requester's own machine (D2), so a task whose worktree is on another
    -- machine is refused rather than given a second worktree here.
    requires: task.host = null or task.host = requester
    -- Redundant with the backlog check while nothing else moves the task, and
    -- not otherwise: a manual move back to backlog during provisioning leaves
    -- the old request open, and a second one beside it would race it.
    requires: not DispatchRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)
    -- D11: only the requester's own owner may name it.
    requires: requester.owner != null and requester.owner = caller()
    -- epics.allium: PhoenixIsNeverChained, enforced where the claim is: the
    -- epic chain's request for a phoenix subtask is refused, with that
    -- reason, like any other refused request. Any other origin may request
    -- a phoenix task.
    requires: not (origin = epic_chain and task.phoenix)

    -- D3: the caller's mode when it names one, else today's derivation.
    let chosen_mode =
        if mode != null: mode
        else: if task.has_plan or task.tag != research: dispatch else: research

    -- The claim, as today: one write, conditional on the checks above.
    ensures: task.status = running
    ensures: task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(running)
    ensures: task.last_pre_tool_use_at = now
    -- The dispatch-claim subagent clear point (agent-health.allium), moved
    -- into the same transaction as the claim.
    ensures:
        for entry in core/SubagentEntries where task = task:
            not exists entry
    ensures: task.live_subagents = 0
    ensures: task.stop_pending = false
    -- The epic status rollup for the claimed task's epic chain, in the same
    -- transaction, as every later move of the request does.
    ensures:
        if task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)
    ensures: DispatchRequest.created(
        task: task,
        kind: dispatch,
        host: requester,
        requested_by: caller(),
        mode: chosen_mode,
        origin: origin,
        state: requested,
        requested_at: now
    )

    @guidance
        -- The request reducer. Every launch path that provisions reaches it
        -- (D6): dispatch.allium's DispatchTask and DispatchResearchTask (the
        -- TUI keys and retry-fresh), tasks.allium's CreateQuickTask,
        -- mcp-task-tools.allium's DispatchTaskViaMcp and epics.allium's
        -- AutoDispatchNextSubtask. Resume paths (ResumeTask, RetryResume)
        -- reuse an existing worktree, take no claim and write no request.
        -- requester is the calling client's host, as the store handle
        -- carries it; a client cannot name some other machine as the host.
        --
        -- A refusal is ORDINARY: it is how a caller learns that another entry
        -- point claimed the task first, as with today's claim_backlog_task.
        -- The refusal names why (not backlog, owned by another machine, an
        -- open request already exists, a phoenix task the epic chain may not
        -- take), and nothing was written. The epic chain treats every refusal
        -- alike and offers its next candidate (epics.allium:
        -- AutoDispatchNextSubtask).
        --
        -- The module also schedules the request's timer here (D4): one row
        -- in a SpacetimeDB schedule table, due at the request's deadline, and
        -- cancelled by whichever rule finishes the request first.
}

rule HostClaimsDispatch {
    when: DispatchRequestDelivered(request)

    requires: request.host = core/local_host()
    requires: request.state = requested
    -- D11: the claim names this host, so only its owner may send it.
    requires: request.host.owner != null and request.host.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = provisioning
    ensures: request.claimed_at = now
    ensures: DispatchProvisioningClaimed(request: request)

    @guidance
        -- DispatchRequestDelivered is the subscription delivering a row that
        -- names this host: a live insert, or one of the initial rows when the
        -- board starts (D5). On the initial rows it runs only after
        -- HostRestartFailsInterrupted has failed the rows that were already
        -- provisioning, so a board never fails its own fresh claim.
        --
        -- The board calls the module's claim reducer, which re-checks
        -- state = requested inside the transaction. Of two boards on the same
        -- host, or of a row delivered twice, exactly one claim is accepted,
        -- and only that board provisions (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch).
        -- A refused claim does nothing further.
        --
        -- Only an ANSWERED claim settles the delivery. A claim that could not
        -- be sent, or a row held back because the board's identity has not
        -- settled (ProvisionDispatch's requested_by check needs it), leaves
        -- the row requested and this board's to claim: the next delivery
        -- serves it again, until the row is claimed or the backstop fails it
        -- at its deadline (DispatchRequestTimesOut).
}

rule HostReportsDispatched {
    when: DispatchProvisioned(request, reporter, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = provisioning
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- D4: a dispatched report is too late once the row's deadline has
    -- passed, by the module's clock. The host has stopped by then
    -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline); a report that raced
    -- it is refused, and the host tears down and reports the failure.
    requires: now < request.deadline

    ensures: request.state = dispatched
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.worktree = worktree
    ensures: request.tmux_window = tmux_window
    ensures: request.reused_worktree = reused_worktree
    -- The writes today's post-provision patch made, now made by the module.
    -- host is written with worktree because core/Task's HostTracksWorktree
    -- invariant pairs the two.
    ensures: request.task.worktree = worktree
    ensures: request.task.tmux_window = tmux_window
    ensures: request.task.host = request.host
    -- The epic status rollup for the task's epic chain, in the same reducer.
    ensures:
        if request.task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(request.task.epic)

    @guidance
        -- Also cancels the request's scheduled timer.
        --
        -- A LATE REPORT IS REFUSED. If the timer has already failed the row,
        -- state is failed and this rule does not apply: the task's fields are
        -- not written, and the claim has already been released. The host
        -- then tears down what it made, by the provisioning-failure rollback
        -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch), so no agent keeps running
        -- against a task that is back in backlog.
        --
        -- A REPEATED REPORT IS ACCEPTED AND CHANGES NOTHING (see
        -- HostRepeatsDispatchedReport): a host whose report got no answer
        -- sends it again, and must hear "accepted" if the first one landed.
}

rule HostRepeatsDispatchedReport {
    when: DispatchProvisioned(request, reporter, worktree, tmux_window, reused_worktree)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = dispatched
    requires: request.worktree = worktree
    requires: request.tmux_window = tmux_window
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    @guidance
        -- Idempotence of HostReportsDispatched: the same report on a row it
        -- already dispatched is accepted with no further writes — not the
        -- row, the task, the epic rollup or the timer — whatever the clock
        -- says now. It is what lets the host settle a dispatched report that
        -- ended in a transport error by simply sending it once more
        -- (dispatch.allium: ProvisionDispatch, "A report that ends in a
        -- transport error"). A report naming a different worktree or window,
        -- or on a row that failed, is still refused.
}

rule HostReportsDispatchFailed {
    when: DispatchProvisioningFailed(request, reporter, reason)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = provisioning
    -- D11.
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = reason

    @guidance
        -- The host has already rolled back what it created (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisionDispatch). Its own deadline stop reports through here too,
        -- with reason "deadline passed" (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline). The claim is released by
        -- ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest, the same rule every failure uses.
        -- Also cancels the request's scheduled timer.
}

rule DispatchRequestTimesOut {
    when: request: DispatchRequest.deadline + config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace <= now

    requires: request.is_open

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = "timed out"

    @guidance
        -- The scheduled reducer (D4), firing from the request's own schedule
        -- row. It is the BACKSTOP: a live host stops at the same deadline and
        -- reports "deadline passed" itself (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisioningStopsAtTheDeadline), which finishes the row and cancels
        -- this timer. It fires config.dispatch_request_backstop_grace after
        -- the deadline, so a live host's stop and rollback land first. What is
        -- left for this rule is a host that never claimed the row (its board
        -- stopped before it claimed) or one that claimed it and is gone or
        -- hung; "timed out" names only that case. Unlike the TUI
        -- watchdog it replaces, it RELEASES
        -- THE CLAIM (through ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest): a request past its
        -- deadline is treated as dead, not slow. That is safe now where it was
        -- not before, because a late dispatched report is refused
        -- (HostReportsDispatched) and the host tears down, so a second
        -- dispatch of the released task cannot meet a first one that still
        -- records its worktree.
}

rule HostRestartFailsInterrupted {
    when: StoreReachedAtStartup(_)

    -- D11: the sweep names this host, so only its owner may run it.
    requires: core/local_host().owner != null and core/local_host().owner = caller()

    for request in DispatchRequests where host = core/local_host() and state = provisioning:
        ensures: DispatchProvisioningFailed(
            request: request,
            reporter: core/local_host(),
            reason: "host restarted"
        )

    @guidance
        -- D5. A row this host had claimed before the board stopped is not
        -- resumed: the process that was provisioning it is gone, and what it
        -- left on disk is unknown. Reporting it failed releases the claim at
        -- once instead of leaving the task claimed until the timer fires.
        -- Fires on the first connection after the board starts
        -- (sync.allium: FirstConnectionLetsTheBoardDraw), never on a
        -- reconnect, when this board's own provisioning may still be running.
        --
        -- ASSUMPTION: ONE BOARD PER HOST. Startup restarts the board inside
        -- the single dispatch tmux session rather than starting a second one
        -- (startup.allium), so the provisioning rows naming this host when a
        -- board starts belong to a board that is no longer running. A second
        -- live board on the same host would have its in-flight requests failed
        -- by this rule; that configuration is not supported.
}

rule DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest {
    when: DeleteTask(task)

    let open = DispatchRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task deleted"

    @guidance
        -- D8. In the SAME reducer that deletes the task row, and it cancels
        -- the request's scheduled timer. Confirmed in scope for every module
        -- reducer that
        -- deletes a task row (DeleteTask and BatchDelete in tasks.allium, and
        -- the stale-subtree delete inside ingest_feed, feeds.allium), not only
        -- the one this trigger names. At most one row matches
        -- (OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask). The host sees its row fail through
        -- its subscription and tears down whatever it made (dispatch.allium:
        -- ProvisionDispatch, "A row that fails under this host"); its later report,
        -- if any, is refused. ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest then finds no task
        -- and does nothing.
}

rule ReleaseClaimOfFailedRequest {
    when: request: DispatchRequest.state transitions_to failed

    let task = request.task
    -- A request failed by its task's deletion (DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenRequest)
    -- has no task left to release.
    let still_claimed = exists task and task.status = running and task.worktree = null

    ensures:
        if still_claimed:
            task.status = backlog
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(backlog)
            task.last_pre_tool_use_at = null
    -- The epic status rollup for the task's epic chain, in the same reducer,
    -- whether or not the release applied. Every failure reaches it: a
    -- reported one, the backstop, a host restart. A deleted task has no chain
    -- left here; its delete rolled the chain up.
    ensures:
        if exists task and task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)

    @guidance
        -- Today's release_backlog_claim, applied by the module to every
        -- failure: a reported one, a timeout, a host restart. It is
        -- conditional, as today: a task that was provisioned or moved on
        -- meanwhile is left alone rather than stomped back to backlog. The
        -- rollup runs in the module so no board has to recalculate an epic
        -- after the module moved its subtask (the backstop and the restart
        -- sweep have no board report behind them to do it). The
        -- last_pre_tool_use_at clear unwinds the stamp RequestDispatch wrote
        -- (the sanctioned exception in agent-health.allium).
}

-- == Session requests: wrap-up and exit-session (M5) ==

rule RequestWrapUp {
    when: WrapUpRequested(task, host, action)

    requires: task.status = running or task.status = review
    requires: task.worktree != null
    -- mcp-task-tools.allium's ReviewTasksAreNotWrappedUp, in the module by
    -- decision (W2): a review-tagged task has no wrap-up to perform. The
    -- refusal carries that guarantee's wording (the tag, that a review task
    -- ends at its PR, and the retag way out). pr_review is core/TaskTag's
    -- value; the store spells it "pr-review", as every tag column does.
    requires: task.tag != pr_review and task.tag != dependabot
    -- The rebase runs where the worktree is, so the row names the task's own
    -- host, and only that host may ask (the MCP endpoint an agent reaches is
    -- its own machine's board).
    requires: task.host = host
    -- D11 (W11): only the host's own owner may name it.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()
    -- W1: at most one open session request per task, of either kind.
    requires: not SessionRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)

    ensures:
        if action = rebase:
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: wrap_up,
                action: action,
                host: host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now
            )
        else:
            -- done and pr: no host work, so the row is written finished, in
            -- this call, and is a valid exit token at once (W2, W3).
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: wrap_up,
                action: action,
                host: host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: done,
                requested_at: now,
                finished_at: now
            )

    @guidance
        -- The request_wrap_up reducer, called by the wrap_up MCP tool
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: WrapUpViaMcp) only after its host-local
        -- checks have passed (W2), so a refusal there wrote nothing. host is
        -- the calling board's own host, as the store handle carries it.
        --
        -- A refusal names why (not running or review, no worktree, a
        -- review-tagged task, the worktree is on another machine, an open
        -- session request already exists) and writes nothing; the tool returns it as an
        -- INVALID_PARAMS error.
        --
        -- No task field is written here. The Conflict clear waits for the
        -- claim (HostClaimsSessionRequest), as today's clear runs just before
        -- the rebase, so a request that is never claimed leaves the task as
        -- it was.
        --
        -- For action rebase the module also schedules the request's timer
        -- here (W7): one row in session_request_timeouts, due at the
        -- deadline plus config.session_request_backstop_grace, and cancelled
        -- by whichever rule finishes the request first. A row written done
        -- has no timer.
}

rule HostClaimsSessionRequest {
    when: SessionRequestDelivered(request)

    requires: request.host = core/local_host()
    requires: request.state = requested
    -- D11 (W11): the claim names this host, so only its owner may send it.
    requires: request.host.owner != null and request.host.owner = caller()
    -- The host enforces the deadline (W7), so a row past it is not started:
    -- the claim is refused, and the backstop fails the row.
    requires: now < request.deadline

    let task = request.task
    -- Bound before the ensures, because an `if` inside ensures reads the
    -- resulting state; the clear tests the sub_status the task had when the
    -- claim arrived.
    let was_conflict = request.kind = wrap_up and task.sub_status = conflict

    ensures: request.state = running
    ensures: request.claimed_at = now
    -- Today's optimistic clear, moved into the claim: the card stops showing
    -- the conflict while the rebase runs (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase).
    ensures:
        if was_conflict:
            task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(task.status)
            task.updated_at = now
    ensures: SessionRequestClaimed(request: request)

    @guidance
        -- SessionRequestDelivered is the subscription delivering a row that
        -- names this host and is requested: a live insert, or one of the
        -- initial rows when the board starts (W8). On the initial rows it
        -- runs only after HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests has
        -- failed the rows that were already running. A wrap_up row written
        -- done is never requested and is never delivered as work.
        --
        -- The claim_session_request reducer re-checks state = requested
        -- inside the transaction, so of two boards, or a row delivered twice,
        -- exactly one claim is accepted, and only that board does the host
        -- work (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase, ExitSessionTeardown). As
        -- for HostClaimsDispatch, only an ANSWERED claim settles the
        -- delivery: an unsent claim leaves the row requested and this
        -- board's to claim again, until the deadline.
}

rule HostReportsSessionRequestDone {
    when: SessionRequestDone(request, reporter)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- As D4: a rebase reported done after the row's deadline, by the module's
    -- clock, is too late, and issues no token. A teardown (an exit_session
    -- or close row) is not held to it: the close stands whatever the
    -- teardown does (W5, X1), so a late teardown report only records what
    -- happened.
    requires: request.kind != wrap_up or now < request.deadline

    ensures: request.state = done
    ensures: request.finished_at = now

    @guidance
        -- report_session_request_done. Also cancels the request's timer. A
        -- done wrap_up row is the exit token from this moment (W3); the
        -- waiting wrap_up call hands its id to the agent
        -- (mcp-task-tools.allium: WrapUpViaMcp).
        --
        -- A LATE REBASE REPORT IS REFUSED: on a row the backstop already
        -- failed, or past the deadline. The rebase did happen in git, and the
        -- host still refreshes its repo-sync state for it
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); the agent, told the wrap-up
        -- failed, calls wrap_up again, and a second rebase of an
        -- already-rebased branch changes nothing.
        --
        -- A REPEATED REPORT IS ACCEPTED AND CHANGES NOTHING
        -- (HostRepeatsSessionRequestDone), so a host whose report got no
        -- answer sends it again, as for HostRepeatsDispatchedReport.
}

rule HostRepeatsSessionRequestDone {
    when: SessionRequestDone(request, reporter)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = done
    -- Only a row this host claimed and reported; a wrap_up row written done
    -- had no report to repeat.
    requires: request.claimed_at != null
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()

    @guidance
        -- Idempotence of HostReportsSessionRequestDone: accepted with no
        -- further writes, whatever the clock says now. A done report on a
        -- row that failed is still refused.
}

rule HostReportsSessionRequestFailed {
    when: SessionRequestFailed(request, reporter, reason, conflict)

    requires: reporter = request.host
    requires: request.state = running
    -- D11 (W11).
    requires: reporter.owner != null and reporter.owner = caller()
    -- Only a rebase can conflict.
    requires: conflict implies request.kind = wrap_up

    let task = request.task

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = reason
    -- THE MODULE sets the Conflict sub_status (W2), through
    -- pr-workflow.allium's FinishTaskConflict, in this transaction. Conflict
    -- is legal only under running and review, so a task moved on meanwhile
    -- (or deleted) is left alone.
    ensures:
        if conflict and exists task and (task.status = running or task.status = review):
            RebaseConflict(task: task)

    @guidance
        -- report_session_request_failed. Also cancels the request's timer.
        -- The host has already aborted a conflicted rebase
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: WrapUpRebase); reason is the host's message,
        -- naming the conflicted files on a conflict. Its own deadline stop
        -- reports through here too, with reason "deadline passed".
        --
        -- A failed wrap_up leaves the task where it was, apart from the
        -- Conflict flag: no status move, no token. The agent fixes the cause
        -- and calls wrap_up again, which writes a new row.
        --
        -- A failed exit_session or close row (the teardown failed) does NOT
        -- revert the close (W5, X1): the task stays Done or Review, and the
        -- row records the reason.
}

rule RequestExitSession {
    when: ExitSessionRequested(task, token, action, pr_url?)

    -- token is the wrap_up row the agent was handed (W3).
    requires: token.task = task
    requires: token.is_valid_token
    -- Part of is_valid_token, stated on its own because it is the newest
    -- condition (W3, decided with the user): a dispatch of the task that
    -- finished at or after the token's row voids the token.
    requires: not DispatchRequests.any(d =>
        d.task = task and d.state = dispatched and d.finished_at >= token.finished_at)
    requires: action = token.action
    requires: task.tmux_window != null
    requires: action = pr implies (pr_url != null and pr_url != "")
    -- D11 (W11): the row names the task's host, whose board tears the window
    -- down, so only that host's owner may ask.
    requires: task.host != null and task.host.owner != null and task.host.owner = caller()
    -- W1: at most one open session request per task. A wrap_up rebase still
    -- running for this task (a second wrap_up the agent started) holds it.
    requires: not SessionRequests.any(r => r.task = task and r.is_open)

    let window = task.tmux_window
    let recorded_pr_url = if action = pr: pr_url else: null

    ensures:
        let close = SessionRequest.created(
            task: task,
            kind: exit_session,
            action: action,
            host: task.host,
            requested_by: caller(),
            state: requested,
            requested_at: now,
            pr_url: recorded_pr_url,
            wrap_up_request: token,
            tmux_window: window
        )
        -- The close itself, in this same transaction: the terminal write,
        -- the epic rollup, the phoenix respawn and the epic chain
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: ExitSession).
        SessionCloseAccepted(task: task, action: action, pr_url: recorded_pr_url, close: close)

    @guidance
        -- The request_exit_session reducer, called by the exit_session MCP
        -- tool (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp). Writing the
        -- exit_session row is what consumes the token: the row names it
        -- (wrap_up_request), and is_valid_token is false from then on. The
        -- module also schedules the row's teardown timer here (W7).
        --
        -- ONE TRANSACTION, SO THE CLOSE IS WHOLE OR ABSENT (W4). A refused
        -- call writes nothing: the task keeps its status and window and the
        -- token stays valid, so the agent may call again once the cause is
        -- fixed. There is no longer a state in which the token is spent but
        -- the close did not land.
        --
        -- Refusals, which the tool returns as INVALID_PARAMS errors carrying
        -- the module's words, in the order the module checks them:
        --   - no unconsumed done wrap_up row for the task (whatever the token
        --     names), or the token's row was already consumed: "no exit
        --     token — call wrap_up first";
        --   - the token names some other row, a done wrap_up row newer
        --     than it exists, or the task was dispatched again after the
        --     token's row finished (a dispatched request of the task whose
        --     finished_at is not earlier, W3): "invalid exit token";
        --   - action differs from the token's: an error naming both actions;
        --   - task.tmux_window is null: "task #<id> has no active session",
        --     which the /wrap-up skill treats as success, as today;
        --   - action pr without a pr_url: "pr_url is required for action
        --     'pr'";
        --   - the host check or an open session request: the reason.
        --
        -- The window to tear down is recorded on the row from the task as
        -- this transaction read it, so the read and the clear are atomic;
        -- the earlier "fresh read just before the patch" race is gone.
}

rule SessionRequestTimesOut {
    when: request: SessionRequest.deadline + config.session_request_backstop_grace <= now

    requires: request.is_open

    ensures: request.state = failed
    ensures: request.finished_at = now
    ensures: request.reason = "timed out"

    @guidance
        -- The scheduled reducer expire_session_request (W7), firing from the
        -- request's own row in session_request_timeouts, refusing any caller
        -- but the module's scheduler. It is the BACKSTOP: a live host stops
        -- at the deadline and reports "deadline passed" itself, which
        -- finishes the row and cancels this timer. What is left is a host
        -- that never claimed the row or is gone or hung.
        --
        -- A timed-out wrap_up leaves the task as it was: a Conflict the claim
        -- cleared is not restored, and none is set. A timed-out exit_session
        -- or close row leaves the close standing (W5, X1); the window may
        -- still be alive (see the open questions).
}

rule HostRestartFailsInterruptedSessionRequests {
    when: StoreReachedAtStartup(_)

    -- D11 (W11): the sweep names this host, so only its owner may run it.
    requires: core/local_host().owner != null and core/local_host().owner = caller()

    for request in SessionRequests where host = core/local_host() and state = running:
        ensures: SessionRequestFailed(
            request: request,
            reporter: core/local_host(),
            reason: "host restarted",
            conflict: false
        )

    @guidance
        -- W8, as D5 and HostRestartFailsInterrupted: the
        -- fail_interrupted_session_requests reducer, on the first connection
        -- after the board starts, never on a reconnect, and on the same
        -- one-board-per-host assumption. A rebase that was running is not
        -- resumed: the process running it is gone, and the worktree may be
        -- mid-rebase. The agent's wrap_up call died with the board, and it
        -- calls wrap_up again. A teardown that was running (an exit_session
        -- or close row) is failed too, and the starting board then kills the
        -- window the row names, best effort (pr-workflow.allium:
        -- KillWindowOfFailedExitSession).
}

rule DeletingATaskFailsItsOpenSessionRequest {
    when: DeleteTask(task)

    let open = SessionRequests where task = task and is_open

    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task deleted"

    @guidance
        -- W9, as D8: in the SAME reducer that deletes the task row, in every
        -- module reducer that deletes one (DeleteTask and BatchDelete in
        -- tasks.allium, the stale-subtree delete inside ingest_feed), and it
        -- cancels the request's timer. At most one row matches
        -- (OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask). A host still rebasing finds its
        -- report refused. An exit_session or close row failed this way before
        -- its teardown ran still has its window killed by its host
        -- (pr-workflow.allium: KillWindowOfFailedExitSession): the close
        -- already cleared the window from the task, so the delete itself does
        -- not.
}

-- == update_tasks' done-path close (M7) ==

rule RequestCloseTask {
    when: CloseTaskRequested(task, host)

    -- No requirement on task.status: reachable from any status, done
    -- included, as MarkTaskDoneViaMcp promises (mcp-task-tools.allium). A
    -- task that no longer exists is refused ("task <id> no longer exists"),
    -- writing nothing. The
    -- request-shape checks (status the only field; a dispatched agent never
    -- closes its own task) are the tool's, made before this call. No host
    -- check guards the close itself: D11 governs only whether the teardown
    -- row is written (X1).

    let was_running = task.status = running
    let was_already_done = task.status = done
    -- Bound before the ensures, because the close clears the window and the
    -- row must name the window as this transaction read it.
    let window = task.tmux_window
    let has_pr_url = task.url != null and task.url.url_type = pr
    -- The machine the window is on: the task's own host, or the caller's
    -- when the task has none (a task never dispatched, with a PR linked by
    -- hand).
    let teardown_host = if task.host != null: task.host else: host
    let needs_teardown = window != null or has_pr_url
    -- D11: a row naming a host makes that host's board act, so only its
    -- owner may write one.
    let may_tear_down = teardown_host.owner != null and teardown_host.owner = caller()
    let open = SessionRequests where task = task and is_open

    -- A stuck wrap_up must not block closing (X1). Failed before the close
    -- row is written, so OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask holds throughout.
    ensures:
        for request in open:
            request.state = failed
            request.finished_at = now
            request.reason = "task closed"

    -- The terminal write: ExitSession's fields for action done
    -- (pr-workflow.allium), with the done -> done exception for completed_at.
    ensures:
        task.status = done
        task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(done)
        task.tmux_window = null
        task.updated_at = now
        if not was_already_done:
            task.completed_at = now
        if was_running:
            task.stop_pending = false

    -- The epic status rollup, in the same transaction.
    ensures:
        if task.epic != null:
            RecalculateEpicStatus(task.epic)

    ensures:
        if needs_teardown and may_tear_down:
            SessionRequest.created(
                task: task,
                kind: close,
                action: done,
                host: teardown_host,
                requested_by: caller(),
                state: requested,
                requested_at: now,
                tmux_window: window
            )

    @guidance
        -- The close_task reducer, called by update_tasks' dedicated
        -- status="done" path (mcp-task-tools.allium: MarkTaskDoneViaMcp)
        -- with this board's own host. Everything above is ONE transaction, so
        -- the close took effect whole or not at all; the board's
        -- close_persisted gate, which guarded a teardown against a close that
        -- had not landed, has nothing left to guard.
        --
        -- NO TOKEN AND NO CHAIN. No exit token is issued, checked or
        -- consumed, and SessionClosed is not emitted, so the epic chain
        -- (epics.allium: AutoDispatchNextSubtask) does not run: only a close
        -- with a wrap_up behind it chains (EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp).
        --
        -- THE PHOENIX RESPAWN. A task that ENTERS Done here respawns, in this
        -- transaction (tasks.allium: PhoenixRespawn, on its usual terms). A
        -- done -> done re-close is no transition, so it neither respawns nor
        -- restamps completed_at.
        --
        -- THE CLOSE ROW. Written only when there is host work: a window to
        -- kill or a pr-typed url to close (pr-workflow.allium: ClosePrOnDone).
        -- A re-close of a task already done that carries a pr-typed url
        -- therefore still writes one, and its host's gh pr close meets a PR
        -- already merged or closed, which logs quietly. The module schedules
        -- the row's teardown timer here (W7, config.exit_session_teardown_timeout).
        -- When the teardown host is not the caller's own (a teammate's
        -- machine, a host with no owner, or one not registered at all) there
        -- is no row, and the close is not refused for it: the task closes
        -- and the window, if any, is left to its own machine, as before M7,
        -- where the board's local kill of such a window failed silently.
        --
        -- FAILING AN OPEN ROW. A wrap_up rebase still running for the task
        -- is failed "task closed"; its host's later report is refused, as for
        -- any failed row. An exit_session or close row still open (an earlier
        -- close whose teardown has not landed) is failed the same way, and
        -- its host kills the window it names (pr-workflow.allium:
        -- KillWindowOfFailedExitSession). The failure is not D11-checked:
        -- the row may name a teammate's machine, and it is the close, not a
        -- request for host work, that fails it.
        --
        -- The tool waits for nothing; its response text is unchanged.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- PR state (M6)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ReportPrStatus {
    when: PrStatusReported(task, host, state, review_decision)

    -- The raw strings gh printed, in any letter case; review_decision is ""
    -- for a repo without branch protection.
    requires: state.upper in { "OPEN", "MERGED", "CLOSED" }
    requires: review_decision.upper in { "", "APPROVED", "CHANGES_REQUESTED", "REVIEW_REQUIRED" }
    -- D11: only the host's own owner may speak for it.
    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()

    ensures:
        -- A report for a task that is gone or no longer in review changes
        -- nothing and is accepted: the host's read raced a close.
        if task.status = review and task.url.url_type = pr:
            if state.upper = "MERGED":
                task.status = done
                task.sub_status = core/SubStatus.default_for(done)
                task.tmux_window = null
                task.completed_at = now
                task.updated_at = now
                -- the epic rollup and the phoenix successor follow, as for
                -- any route into done
            else if state.upper = "CLOSED":
                if task.sub_status != pr_closed:
                    task.sub_status = pr_closed
                    task.updated_at = now
            else if task.sub_status != conflict:
                let new_sub =
                    if review_decision.upper = "APPROVED": approved
                    else if review_decision.upper = "CHANGES_REQUESTED": changes_requested
                    else: awaiting_review
                if task.sub_status != new_sub:
                    task.sub_status = new_sub
                    task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The report_pr_status reducer. It replaces the board's own reading
        -- of gh output (PollPrStatus, PrMerged, PrClosed in
        -- pr-workflow.allium). An unknown state or review decision is a
        -- refusal that writes nothing; the host counts any refusal as one
        -- permanent read failure toward the give-up threshold (P3), and a
        -- reducer call that fails in transport as a transient one.
        -- Repeating the same report is a no-op.
        -- The host that polls a host-less task is chosen by core/PollOwner,
        -- as before; the module does not check ownership of the poll, only
        -- that the caller owns the host it names.
        -- A closed PR overrides conflict, as before: it is the stronger
        -- signal. An open one never does.
}

rule ReportPrUnreadable {
    when: PrUnreadableReported(task, host, reason)

    requires: host.owner != null and host.owner = caller()

    ensures:
        if task.status = review and task.url.url_type = pr
           and task.sub_status != pr_unreachable:
            task.sub_status = pr_unreachable
            task.updated_at = now

    @guidance
        -- The report_pr_unreadable reducer. The host counts consecutive
        -- permanent failures (P3) and posts this once, at the threshold
        -- (core/config.pr_poll_permanent_failure_threshold). reason is for
        -- the log line only; nothing stores it. A later successful
        -- report_pr_status clears pr_unreachable through its open branch,
        -- merged branch or closed branch, as before.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- D1: a task holds at most one open request. RequestDispatch refuses a second
-- one while the first is open.
invariant OneOpenDispatchRequestPerTask {
    for a in DispatchRequests:
        for b in DispatchRequests:
            a != b and a.task = b.task implies not (a.is_open and b.is_open)
}

-- W1: a task holds at most one open session request, of either kind.
-- RequestWrapUp and RequestExitSession refuse a second one while the first is
-- open.
invariant OneOpenSessionRequestPerTask {
    for a in SessionRequests:
        for b in SessionRequests:
            a != b and a.task = b.task implies not (a.is_open and b.is_open)
}

-- W3: an exit token is consumed once. No two exit_session rows name the same
-- wrap_up row.
invariant AnExitTokenIsConsumedOnce {
    for a in SessionRequests:
        for b in SessionRequests:
            a != b and a.kind = exit_session and b.kind = exit_session
                implies a.wrap_up_request != b.wrap_up_request
}

-- W4: every exit_session row consumed a done wrap_up row of its own task, so
-- every close through exit_session had a wrap_up behind it. It applies to
-- exit_session rows ONLY (X1): a close row is a close with no wrap_up behind
-- it, by design, and CloseRowsCarryNoTokenAndNoChain below states what it
-- carries instead.
invariant EveryExitSessionHadAWrapUp {
    for r in SessionRequests:
        r.kind = exit_session implies (
            r.wrap_up_request != null
            and r.wrap_up_request.kind = wrap_up
            and r.wrap_up_request.state = done
            and r.wrap_up_request.task = r.task)
}

-- X1: a close row consumed no token and ran no chain, and its action is
-- always done.
invariant CloseRowsCarryNoTokenAndNoChain {
    for r in SessionRequests:
        r.kind = close implies (
            r.action = done
            and r.wrap_up_request = null
            and r.chained_task = null
            and r.pr_url = null)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Resolved in M7 (task #53260), recorded as decisions in the header rather
-- than kept here: how update_tasks' status="done" path moves into the module
-- (X1), and ClosePrOnDone's owner on the module's close routes ("ClosePrOnDone's
-- owner" under M7: the host serving the teardown row; a row that fails before
-- its host serves it leaves the PR open, accepted as built and flagged for
-- user review).

open question "Retention of finished request rows (deferred past M7, and so beyond the store-split epic): dispatched and failed dispatch rows, and done and failed session rows, stay as history and their host's subscription delivers all of them. How long are they kept, and what removes them? A done wrap_up row that is still a valid exit token must outlive any cleanup."

open question "Window left alive (W5, W7, W8; deferred past M7, and so beyond the store-split epic): an exit_session or close row the backstop fails because its board was closed or hung, or one the restart sweep fails, may leave the agent's tmux window alive on a task that is already Done or Review. The spec has the starting board kill the window of a row its own sweep failed, and a host kill the window of a row failed by deletion. Should a starting board also kill the windows of teardown rows that failed 'timed out' while it was not running (W8 otherwise treats them as history, as D12 does), or is a surviving window acceptable?"

open question "Seeing other machines' requests (deferred past M7, and so beyond the store-split epic): a board's subscription delivers only request rows naming its own host. A teammate's board that shows the same task in a shared epic cannot see the request, so its UnprovisionedIndicator falls back to the claim stamp. Should boards also receive the open requests of every task they can see?"

open question "M7, close_task on a task with an open DISPATCH request (not decided in M7): RequestCloseTask fails the task's open session request but not an open dispatch request. A task closed while its dispatch is still provisioning can then be reported dispatched, and HostReportsDispatched writes a worktree, window and host onto a Done task, with an agent running in it. The board-side close before M7 had the same gap. Should close_task also fail the task's open dispatch request ('task closed'), as a task delete does (D8), or refuse the close while one is open?"

open question "M7, update_tasks' done path when close_task gets NO ANSWER (found by weed, not decided): the board treats a no-answer like a refusal and the tool replies that the task could NOT be moved to done and is still in its previous status. The close may in fact have landed. A retry is a done -> done re-close (no restamp, no respawn), but for a task with a pr-typed url it writes a second close row and runs gh pr close again. Should the tool settle a no-answer by re-reading the task, as exit_session settles its own no-answer by looking for the row (mcp-task-tools.allium: ExitSessionViaMcp), or is the 'try again' reply acceptable?"

open question "M7 leftovers not named in X5 (found by weed, not decided): besides the plain upsert_feed_tasks reducer, the create_managed_role_epic and create_repo_group_sub_epic reducers and the store's recalculate_epic_status call are now reached only from tests (ProvisionManagedEpics runs through ensure_managed_epics, repo-group routing runs inside the module, and X3 removed every board-side recalculation). Should M7 delete them, or should X5 list them as deferred alongside upsert_feed_tasks?"
